1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,376
Your backtest and your live system are usually two different codebases.

2
00:00:03,426 --> 00:00:04,908
That's why they drift.

3
00:00:05,250 --> 00:00:07,512
With Wingfoil you describe it once, in Rust.

4
00:00:07,562 --> 00:00:11,867
For example: split a counter into odds and evens, merge them back, then run it.

5
00:00:11,917 --> 00:00:13,412
Under the hood it's a graph.

6
00:00:13,462 --> 00:00:17,600
The engine runs each node once per cycle, even where branches recombine.

7
00:00:17,650 --> 00:00:21,700
Run it historically, and it's an instant, deterministic replay for backtests.

8
00:00:21,750 --> 00:00:24,558
Run the same graph live, and it's paced by the wall clock.

9
00:00:24,608 --> 00:00:26,150
Same values, same order.

10
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:27,933
Only the clock changes.

11
00:00:27,983 --> 00:00:29,142
One definition.

12
00:00:29,192 --> 00:00:31,801
A backtest and a live system that can't drift.

13
00:00:32,317 --> 00:00:33,278
Wingfoil.

14
00:00:33,328 --> 00:00:35,306
Stream processing in Rust.
